  "response": "## Missing Agent Context: confirmed\n\nThe scan reports the finding directly:\n\n- **`crime_00004` · Missing Agent Context**: severity medium, confidence 0.90, effort small.\n- The repo root has no `AGENTS.md` and no `CLAUDE.md`.\n- There are no `.claude/skills/*/SKILL.md` or `.agents/skills/*/SKILL.md` files.\n- `package.json` declares two binaries, `tsup` and `tsup-node`, but nothing tells an agent how to build, test or run them.\n\nThe finding is attached to `assets/cjs_shims.js`, but that's just where the scanner pinned it; the file has nothing to do with it. The problem covers the whole repo, and the fingerprint (`missing_agent_context::assets/cjs_shims.js::`) is what you'll see in diffs.\n\n## What this means for agent work here\n\nAn agent opening this repo starts with nothing written down and has to guess everything from the code. The other 73 findings show where that goes wrong:\n\n1. **It won't know which code is load-bearing.** `src/utils.ts` has 18 direct importers and 26 transitive ones, which is the largest blast radius in the repo. It is also 424 lines long, and its `jsoncParse` turns any parse failure into `{}` without a trace. `src/log.ts` (9 importers) and `src/plugin.ts` (10 importers, mostly type-only) are also widely shared. Without a note, an agent will treat these like any other file.\n2. **It will trust tests that don't check anything.** Seven tests have no assertions at all, including both `test/graphql.test.ts` tests, both `test/example.test.ts` tests, and `custom tsconfig`. Another 13 use only weak matchers. A green run tells an agent less than it thinks, and nothing in the repo says so.\n3. **Some names hide side effects.** `getRollupConfig`, `build`, `buildAll` and `buildStart` all call `removeFiles`. An agent that reads `getRollupConfig` as a pure read will call it in the wrong place.\n4. **Build failures are partly hidden.** The `catch` blocks in `runTypeScriptCompiler`, `runDtsRollup` and `runRollup` log a message but drop the error itself. An agent debugging the dts or rollup paths will see the error message but lose its stack, cause and code.\n5. **It can't find the commands.** There are two binaries and no documented build, test or typecheck steps, so the agent guesses or browses `package.json` every session.\n\nNone of these is fixed by the AGENTS.md itself. The file just lets the next agent start out knowing about them.\n\n## Minimal AGENTS.md skeleton\n\nAny one of the listed files clears the finding. The scanner's `fix_shape` suggests a SKILL.md, but the `suggested_actions` entry accepts AGENTS.md as well. I'd go with AGENTS.md because it's read by the most agents. `<TODO>` marks things the scan doesn't show; take those from `package.json` `scripts` rather than guessing.\n\n```markdown\n# AGENTS.md\n\nThis is the bundler behind the `tsup` and `tsup-node` CLIs (see `package.json` `bin`).\n\n## Commands\n- Install: `<TODO: package manager install command>`\n- Build: `<TODO: from package.json scripts>`\n- Test: `<TODO: from package.json scripts>` (tests live in `test/*.test.ts`)\n- Typecheck: `<TODO>`\n\nRun test and typecheck before you open a PR.\n\n## Layout\n- `src/cli-main.ts`: parses CLI flags (`main`)\n- `src/index.ts`: `normalizeOptions` and `build` orchestration (watcher, buildAll, mainTasks)\n- `src/esbuild/`: esbuild runner and plugins (postcss, svelte, swc)\n- `src/rollup.ts`, `src/rollup/`, `src/tsc.ts`, `src/api-extractor.ts`: the dts paths\n- `src/plugins/`: post-build plugins (terser, swc-target, cjs-splitting)\n- `assets/`: shims injected into output (e.g. `cjs_shims.js`)\n- `test/utils.ts`: shared test harness (9 test files import it)\n\n## Load-bearing files: read their importers before editing\n- `src/utils.ts`: 18 direct importers. `jsoncParse` returns `{}` when parsing fails.\n- `src/log.ts`: 9 importers\n- `src/plugin.ts`: shared plugin types, 10 importers\n\n## Known traps\n- `getRollupConfig`, `build`, `buildAll` and `buildStart` delete files (`removeFiles`).\n  Don't call them as if they were pure.\n- The catch blocks in `runTypeScriptCompiler`, `runDtsRollup` and `runRollup` log a message\n  but drop the error. Look for the real cause before you trust the log line.\n- Several tests have no assertions or only weak ones (`test/graphql.test.ts`,\n  `test/example.test.ts`, parts of `test/index.test.ts`, `test/tsconfig.test.ts`,\n  `test/dts.test.ts`). A passing suite doesn't prove behaviour is unchanged.\n  New tests must assert on the output files or their contents.\n\n## Conventions\n- `<TODO: formatting/lint, commit style, changeset/release process>`\n```\n\n## Before you commit it\n\n- **Fill in the `<TODO>`s from `package.json`.** I worked only from the scan output and didn't read `package.json`, so I left the commands blank rather than guess them.\n- **Check that the finding is gone.** Re-run `crimes scan --format json` and confirm the `missing_agent_context::assets/cjs_shims.js::` fingerprint no longer appears.\n- **Check the \"monorepo\" assumption.** The scan's package breakdown lists only `assets` and `test`, so it saw one package, not a workspace. If there really are several packages, add a short section per package or a nested `AGENTS.md` for each.\n- **Consider a separate PR.** It's a small, low-risk change, and keeping it apart from your first code PR makes both easier to review.",
